        "title": "feat(signal): add setup wizard",
        "body": "## What Problem This Solves\n\nSignal setup needs a guided path that proves a usable receive transport before users rely on the channel, without OpenClaw creating or managing a Signal Docker container. The prior setup surface could also leave stale transport settings in config, report ambiguous probe state, or persist partial setup results after a failed/cancelled setup attempt.\n\n## Why This Change Was Made\n\nSignal setup now stays owned by the Signal plugin: users can configure a local `signal-cli` native daemon or connect OpenClaw to an existing Signal server they manage. Core setup only gains the generic `setupApplied: false` rollback signal needed to avoid selection hooks, callbacks, completion notes, and config persistence for unsuccessful setup attempts. Existing/manual `bbernhard/signal-cli-rest-api` servers remain supported through `apiMode: \"container\"` and receive-readiness probes, but the wizard no longer offers an OpenClaw-managed Docker container path.\n\n## User Impact\n\nUsers get Signal setup prompts for native `signal-cli` or an existing server URL, with account-aware receive probing before setup is accepted. Operators get clearer `channels status --probe` output for ready, account-missing, receive-unavailable, and unreachable Signal states. Multi-account Signal configs can scope `apiMode` per account while preserving channel-level `channels.signal.apiMode` as the default.\n\n## Evidence\n\nBehavioral proof:\n\n- Existing-server setup prompts for the Signal phone number, probes the provided server with that account, and saves `httpUrl`, `autoStart: false`, and `apiMode: \"auto\"` only after the probe succeeds.\n- Native setup clears stale external/container transport fields and saves native daemon settings for the intended account.\n- Existing/manual container servers still validate `/v1/accounts` with bounded reads and require `/v1/receive/{account}` WebSocket readiness when receive capability is required.\n- Failed or cancelled setup can return `setupApplied: false`, so core setup rolls back partial config and skips account-selection side effects.\n- Approval reaction hints are only appended when a configured Signal account id exists, avoiding hints that cannot be registered for explicit `baseUrl` plus `account` sends.\n- Shared setup prompt wording was left unchanged; the shared setup diff is limited to the rollback result contract/pass-through plus a Signal `apiMode` promotion regression test.\n- Screenshots are omitted because this PR changes terminal/setup wizard, gateway status, config, and docs behavior; command/test output is the reviewer-checkable proof.\n\nAutomated and review checks on head `2cb81aab8cc429b239f05fddbdce84a3eb06a2a9`:\n\n- `node scripts/run-vitest.mjs src/channels/plugins/setup-helpers.test.ts src/flows/channel-setup.test.ts extensions/signal/src/core.test.ts extensions/signal/src/setup-core.test.ts extensions/signal/src/client-container.test.ts extensions/signal/src/send.test.ts` passed: 6 files, 209 tests.\n- `git diff --check origin/main...HEAD` passed.\n- `corepack pnpm config:docs:check` passed.\n- `node --import tsx scripts/generate-bundled-channel-config-metadata.ts --check` passed.\n- `corepack pnpm plugin-sdk:api:check` passed after regenerating the expected Plugin SDK API baseline hash for the additive setup result field.\n\nEarlier review/proof on the prior same-feature head found no implementation blockers, but GitHub CI and ClawSweeper are rerunning for this updated head after removing the out-of-scope shared prompt-copy change.\n\nKnown proof limits:\n\n- Focused validation used narrow local wrappers because earlier Testbox hydration/DNS failed in this checkout. No manual Signal messages were sent, and no real `~/.openclaw/openclaw.json` config was touched.\n",
